and grow_arrays() left those views marked host-modified (:1728-1732), so that sync pushes create_bodies()' real bodytag data to the device; the pack/copylist scan (:1886-1943) and unpack_exchange_kokkos (:2072-2136) then migrate it on the device and mark the views device-modified (:1988-1998, :2141-2150).  VerletKokkos::setup() (src/KOKKOS/verlet_kokkos.cpp) runs comm->exchange() and atom->sort() back to back and only afterwards modify->setup_pre_neighbor(), so on run 1 both clear sites drop a device claim that is real, and the following `k_bodytag.template sync<LMPDeviceType>()` (:2742) pushes the stale host copy back over it.

src/KOKKOS/fix_rigid_small_kokkos.cpp:123-127:
  if (extended) {
    memoryKK->destroy_kokkos(k_eflags, eflags);
    if (orientflag) memoryKK->destroy_kokkos(k_orient, orient);
    if (dorientflag) memoryKK->destroy_kokkos(k_dorient, dorient);
  }
destroy_kokkos() is what nulls the base pointer (src/KOKKOS/memory_kokkos.h:83-88 and :271-281), and that nulling is exactly what makes the base destructor's memory->destroy(eflags)/orient/dorient (src/RIGID/fix_rigid_small.cpp:498-500) a no-op.  But eflags/orient/dorient are allocated in grow_arrays (fix_rigid_small_kokkos.cpp:1670-1677) with the flag values current at that time, and src/RIGID/fix_rigid_small.cpp:1777 `extended = orientflag = dorientflag = 0;` re-derives all three at the start of every setup_bodies_static(), which reinitflag re-runs on each subsequent run.
